# Source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2" (solved), 3 c).
id: integer-square-root
title: Integer square root: refining a candidate
topic: Loop invariants
level: 3
source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 3 c
tags: invariant, failed candidate, refine, disjunction

task:
  The loop finds the smallest $i$ whose square is larger than $n$. The
  solution sheet proposes the invariant
  $s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$. Check it, and repair it
  if a check fails.

pre: n >= 0 && s = 0 && i = 0
post: i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n < i * i

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (s <= n) do {
    {I && s <= n}
    {R2}
    i := i + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    s := i * i       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(s <= n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Check establishment of the proposed candidate
rule: LOGIC
vc: n >= 0 && s = 0 && i = 0 ==> s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n
status: fails
counterexample: n = 0, s = 0, i = 0
why:
  At the start $i = 0$, so $(i - 1) * (i - 1) = 1$. With $n = 0$ the
  conjunct $1 <= 0$ is false. The candidate rejects a real start state, so
  it is too strong.

step: Repair the candidate
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The conjunct $(i - 1)^2 <= n$ says that the previous value of $i$ was
  small enough. Before the first iteration there is no previous value, so
  we only require it when $i != 0$. We also keep $n >= 0$, which the exit
  check will need.
set: I = s = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)
vc: n >= 0 && s = 0 && i = 0 ==> I
why:
  $s = 0 = 0 * 0$, $0 <= 0$, $0 <= n$, and the disjunction holds by its
  first part $i = 0$.

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(s := i * i, I)
  = i * i = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)
  = 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)
set: R1 = 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i + 1 = 0 || (i + 1 - 1) * (i + 1 - 1) <= n)
  = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i = -1 || i * i <= n)
set: R2 = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i = -1 || i * i <= n)
vc: I && s <= n ==> R2
why:
  From $0 <= i$ we get $0 <= i + 1$. The second disjunct holds:
  $i * i = s$ by the invariant, and $s <= n$ by the condition of the loop.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: I && !(s <= n) ==> i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n < i * i
why:
  $!(s <= n)$ gives $n < s = i * i$. For $(i - 1)^2 <= n$, we look at the
  disjunction of the invariant. If $i = 0$ then $s = 0$, and $n < 0$
  contradicts $0 <= n$; so this case cannot happen at the exit, and the
  other disjunct $(i - 1)^2 <= n$ holds.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The failed establishment check gave a concrete state, $n = 0$, $i = 0$,
  $s = 0$, that the candidate wrongly rejected. The repair weakens the
  candidate exactly on that state, with the disjunct $i = 0$. A variant for
  termination is $n - i$: inside the loop $i <= i * i = s <= n$.
